\title{Psychometric Analysis Reveals Structural Benchmark Instability\\That Contamination Validation Cannot Detect}

\author{Anonymous}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

%==============================================================================
\begin{abstract}
LLM benchmarks implicitly assume temporal score comparability---that a score on MMLU today measures the same construct as a score measured six months ago. We apply Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\times$ 12,508 MMLU items) and find that 28--30\% of items are functionally unstable across model generations, even within architecture-homogeneous decoder-only populations where IRT model fit is excellent (RMSEA$=$0.024, CFI$=$0.966). Instability decomposition shows that partial $\rho=0.22$ ($p=0.012$) persists after controlling for domain accuracy improvement, indicating genuine item function change beyond aggregate capability gains. We demonstrate that the field's standard validation approach---web-overlap contamination labels---cannot capture this differential signal (enrichment$\approx$1.0), while a semi-synthetic injection framework confirms detection capability ($\Delta$TPR$=$+0.508 on MMLU, +0.818 on GSM8K). Cross-subject matching eliminates false positive inflation ($\Delta$FPR$=$0.000). Group-level DIF statistics remain robust to reasoning-RL model compositions (flag retention 89--91\%, AUROC$=$0.88) while traditional per-model detectors degrade significantly (ConStat AUROC: 0.73$\to$0.54; Min-K\%: 0.68$\to$0.51). We further characterize DIF via complementary methods (ConStat perturbation, generation variance, ensemble fusion), continuous monitoring extensions (Item-Focused Trees, cumulative Bayesian DIF), and a principled equating framework for benchmark maintenance. Our findings reveal that psychometric analysis exposes structural benchmark problems invisible to existing contamination detection methodology.
\end{abstract}

%==============================================================================
\section{Introduction}

When we say ``Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,'' we implicitly assume both scores reflect the same latent construct. This assumption of temporal score comparability is foundational to benchmark-based evaluation yet rarely tested. If items function differently across model generations---some becoming trivially easy due to training data overlap, others shifting in cognitive demand as architectures evolve---then benchmark scores from different periods are not directly comparable.

We bring Differential Item Functioning (DIF), a classical psychometric tool with decades of validation in educational testing, to characterize this temporal instability at unprecedented scale. Using the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\times$ 12,508 MMLU items), we conduct the largest DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date.

Our contributions are fourfold:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Temporal instability characterization}: 28--30\% of MMLU items exhibit large DIF (ETS C category, $|\Delta_\text{MH}|\geq 1.5$) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts, persisting within architecture-homogeneous decoder-only populations with excellent IRT fit (RMSEA$=$0.024). Instability decomposition reveals genuine item function change (partial $\rho=0.22$, $p=0.012$) beyond domain-level capability gains.

\item \textbf{Ground truth meta-critique}: Web-overlap contamination labels measure global exposure (whether an item is publicly accessible), not differential contamination (whether an item functions differently across cohorts). Enrichment$\approx$1.0 between DIF flags and web-overlap labels is expected, not failure.

\item \textbf{Semi-synthetic validation framework}: A reusable $\Delta$TPR methodology with cross-subject matching ($\Delta$FPR$=$0.000) for validating any item-level contamination detector.

\item \textbf{DIF as diagnostic lens}: MMLU rankings exhibit massive measurement redundancy ($\rho\approx$0.997 after 31\% removal), yet DIF items concentrate cross-cohort discriminative signal. Cross-benchmark validation on GSM8K shows DIF items resist contamination-based gains ($r=-0.478$, $p=0.005$).
\end{enumerate}

We further demonstrate DIF's robustness to reasoning-RL compositions, compare with complementary detection methods, and sketch principled extensions toward longitudinal benchmark monitoring and equating-based maintenance.

%==============================================================================
\section{Background: Differential Item Functioning}

DIF occurs when examinees of equal ability from different groups have unequal probabilities of answering an item correctly. In our setting, the ``groups'' are temporal cohorts of LLM models (reference: 2023 models; focal: 2024-H1 models), and ``ability'' is total benchmark score.

\paragraph{Mantel-Haenszel statistic.} We use the MH odds ratio $\alpha_\text{MH}$ and its log-transform $\Delta_\text{MH} = -2.35 \ln(\alpha_\text{MH})$, classified via ETS categories: A ($|\Delta_\text{MH}|<1.0$, negligible), B ($1.0\leq|\Delta_\text{MH}|<1.5$, moderate), C ($|\Delta_\text{MH}|\geq 1.5$, large). An item flagged C exhibits functionally meaningful differential behavior between cohorts after conditioning on total ability.

\paragraph{IRT model fit.} We assess measurement model adequacy via confirmatory factor analysis fit indices: RMSEA ($<$0.05 acceptable, $<$0.03 excellent), CFI ($>$0.95 good), TLI ($>$0.95 good), and SRMR ($<$0.05 good). These validate the unidimensionality assumption underlying MH-DIF.

%==============================================================================
\section{Architecture-Stratified DIF Analysis}\label{sec:arch}

The strongest potential objection to temporal DIF findings is that architecture heterogeneity (mixing decoder-only, encoder-decoder, and hybrid models) violates IRT's unidimensionality assumption, producing spurious DIF. We eliminate this concern by stratifying to decoder-only models.

\subsection{Experimental Setup}

From the full METABENCH population ($N=5,219$), we extract decoder-only models ($N=3,487$) sharing transformer architecture, autoregressive training, and similar tokenizer families. Reference cohort: 892 models (released $\leq$2023); focal cohort: 513 models (released 2024-H1).

\subsection{IRT Model Fit}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{IRT fit comparison: full population vs.\ decoder-only subset.}
\label{tab:irt_fit}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Population & $N$ & RMSEA & CFI & TLI & SRMR \\
\midrule
Full & 5,219 & 0.067 & 0.874 & 0.851 & 0.058 \\
Decoder-only & 3,487 & 0.024 & 0.966 & 0.958 & 0.031 \\
Encoder-decoder & 412 & 0.041 & 0.921 & 0.903 & 0.044 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

RMSEA improves by 64.2\% (0.067$\to$0.024) when restricting to decoder-only models, with CFI and TLI both exceeding 0.95. The measurement model is well-specified for this population: the latent trait is close to unidimensional within decoder-only models, which vary primarily along a single capability axis (scale $\times$ data $\times$ training quality).

\subsection{Temporal DIF Persistence}

Despite excellent model fit, temporal DIF persists at 29.1\% (C category) in the decoder-only population---only a 2.2 percentage point reduction from the full population's 31.3\%. Random-split sanity check yields 0.14\% C items. Architecture mixing contributed $\sim$2pp of spurious DIF; the remaining 29\% is genuine signal.

\subsection{Instability Decomposition}

A natural concern is that item instability simply reflects ``models got better at those domains.'' We decompose temporal DIF variance:

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Instability decomposition: DIF variance attribution.}
\label{tab:decomposition}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Predictor & $\rho$ & $p$ & Variance explained (\%) \\
\midrule
Domain accuracy improvement & 0.307 & 0.020 & 9.4 \\
Partial $\rho$ (residual) & 0.220 & 0.012 & 4.8 \\
Total temporal C variance & --- & --- & 100.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Domain-level accuracy improvement explains 9.4\% of item instability variance. After controlling for this, partial $\rho=0.22$ ($p=0.012$) remains significant: DIF captures genuine item function change---shifts in distractor attractiveness, reasoning chain difficulty, or cognitive demand---beyond aggregate capability gains.

\subsection{Non-Uniform DIF Robustness Check}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Non-uniform DIF method comparison (8 subjects tested).}
\label{tab:nonuniform}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Method & C flags & Overlap with MH (\%) & Unique flags (\%) \\
\midrule
MH (standard) & 3,627 & 100.0 & 26.2 \\
SIBTEST & 2,814 & 78.0 & 4.2 \\
Logistic regression & 2,951 & 81.3 & 3.8 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

78\% of SIBTEST flags overlap with MH, confirming MH captures the dominant uniform DIF pattern. The median $R^2$ for the logistic regression interaction term is 0.008---non-uniform DIF exists but is secondary. MH is adequate for this population.

%==============================================================================
\section{Robustness to Reasoning-RL Compositions}\label{sec:rl}

A practical concern for DIF deployment is robustness to novel training paradigms, particularly reinforcement learning from reasoning (GRPO, PPO). We compare DIF's group-level robustness against traditional per-model detectors.

\subsection{DIF Flag Stability}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{DIF flag retention across cohort compositions.}
\label{tab:rl_stability}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Cohort & $N$ & Temporal C\% & Flag retention & 95\% CI \\
\midrule
SFT-only & 1,843 & 30.8 & 1.00 & --- \\
Alignment-RL & 477 & 32.1 & 0.91 & [0.87, 0.95] \\
SFT + reasoning-RL & 1,856 & 31.0 & 0.89 & [0.84, 0.94] \\
All combined & 2,333 & 31.4 & 0.90 & [0.85, 0.94] \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

DIF flags remain 89--91\% stable when reasoning-RL models are added to the focal group. This stability follows from DIF's design: as a group-level statistic computed over entire cohorts, adding 13 reasoning-RL models to a focal group of $\sim$500 changes composition by $<$3\%.

\subsection{Comparison with Traditional Detectors}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Traditional detector degradation on reasoning-RL models.}
\label{tab:rl_detectors}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Detector & AUROC (non-RL) & AUROC (reasoning-RL) & $\Delta$ & DeLong $p$ \\
\midrule
ConStat & 0.73 & 0.54 & $-$0.19 & 0.003 \\
Min-K\% & 0.68 & 0.51 & $-$0.17 & 0.008 \\
DIF (group flag) & 0.88 & 0.86 & $-$0.02 & 0.71 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Traditional detectors operating at the individual model level degrade significantly on reasoning-RL models (AUROC drops of 0.17--0.19, DeLong $p<0.01$). Reasoning-RL training with PPO/GRPO contracts the NLL gap between memorized and non-memorized items, directly degrading per-model distributional signatures. DIF's group-level conditioning makes it agnostic to individual training procedures.

\paragraph{Limitations.} The reasoning-RL sample is small ($N=13$). Bootstrap CIs are wide [0.84, 0.94]. We report this as supporting evidence with explicit caveats.

%==============================================================================
\section{Complementary Detection Methods}\label{sec:complementary}

While DIF is our primary tool, we characterize the detection landscape through complementary methods operating on orthogonal signals.

\subsection{ConStat Perturbation Testing}

ConStat detects contamination by measuring performance drop on semantically equivalent reformulations. We reformulate 237 MMLU items and test 42 non-RL and 11 reasoning-RL models.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{ConStat detection power by model type and contamination type.}
\label{tab:constat}
\begin{tabular}{llccc}
\toprule
Model type & Contam.\ type & AUROC & TPR@FPR=0.10 & $N$ \\
\midrule
Non-RL & Verbatim & 0.74 & 0.42 & 42 \\
Non-RL & Variant & 0.61 & 0.24 & 42 \\
Reasoning-RL & Verbatim & 0.55 & 0.18 & 11 \\
Reasoning-RL & Variant & 0.48 & 0.09 & 11 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

ConStat works well on non-RL models (AUROC$=$0.74 for verbatim contamination) where memorization creates a sharp original-vs-reformulation gap. On reasoning-RL models, PPO/GRPO generalizes memorized knowledge to nearby phrasings, collapsing the perturbation signal. Jaccard overlap between ConStat and DIF flags is only 0.18---the methods capture fundamentally different contamination dimensions.

\subsection{DVD Generation Variance Analysis}

DVD exploits generation-time behavioral signatures: memorized items produce consistently correct answers across temperature samples (low variance). We test 12 open-weight models with 50 responses per item ($T=0.8$) across 500 items.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{DVD generation variance statistics by item category.}
\label{tab:dvd}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Item category & Mean variance & Std & Bimodal (\%) \\
\midrule
Clean items & 0.34 & 0.12 & 3.1 \\
DIF-flagged items & 0.29 & 0.15 & 8.7 \\
Injected verbatim & 0.18 & 0.09 & 12.4 \\
Injected variant & 0.31 & 0.13 & 5.2 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Contaminated items show roughly half the generation variance of clean items (ratio$=$1.89). DVD achieves AUROC$=$0.67 for verbatim contamination but drops to 0.53 for variant contamination. Jaccard overlap with DIF is 0.13---DVD captures a unique signal dimension inaccessible to group-level statistics. Total cost: 58 GPU-hours.

\subsection{Multi-Signal Ensemble Fusion}

We fuse DIF, ConStat, and DVD via majority voting and calibrated weighted fusion.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Ensemble performance comparison.}
\label{tab:ensemble}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Strategy & FPR & TPR (verbatim) & TPR (variant) & AUROC \\
\midrule
DIF only & 0.04 & 0.48 & 0.12 & 0.76 \\
ConStat only & 0.06 & 0.42 & 0.24 & 0.71 \\
DVD only & 0.08 & 0.28 & 0.19 & 0.64 \\
Majority (2/3) & 0.07 & 0.58 & 0.34 & 0.79 \\
Weighted fusion & 0.06 & 0.64 & 0.39 & 0.82 \\
Uniform weight & 0.07 & 0.61 & 0.36 & 0.79 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Weighted fusion (DIF: 0.45, ConStat: 0.35, DVD: 0.20) achieves AUROC$=$0.82, a +0.06 gain over DIF alone. The three detectors exploit orthogonal signals: group-level response patterns (DIF), per-model perturbation sensitivity (ConStat), and generation variance (DVD). Cross-validation confirms stability (5-fold AUROC std$=$0.03). Variant contamination remains the hardest case (TPR$=$0.39).

%==============================================================================
\section{Continuous Monitoring Extensions}\label{sec:monitoring}

\subsection{Item-Focused Trees}

Item-Focused Trees (IFT) treat model release date as a continuous covariate, avoiding the arbitrary 2023/2024-H1 categorical boundary.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{IFT vs.\ categorical DIF detection comparison.}
\label{tab:ift}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Method & Significant (\%) & Unique (\%) & FPR & Power ($\delta$=1.4) & $\kappa$ \\
\midrule
Categorical MH & 31.3 & 2.4 & 0.042 & 0.68 & --- \\
IFT (continuous) & 34.7 & 5.8 & 0.041 & 0.72 & 0.74 \\
Intersection & 28.9 & --- & --- & --- & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

81.2\% overlap between IFT and categorical DIF validates the temporal boundary choice. IFT reveals additional nuance: 76.1\% of significant items show abrupt change patterns (sudden capability jumps at specific model generations), while 23.9\% exhibit gradual drift (continuous capability improvement). The modal optimal split date is 2023-09, close to our categorical cutoff.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{IFT drift pattern classification.}
\label{tab:ift_patterns}
\begin{tabular}{lcll}
\toprule
Pattern & \% of significant & Example domain & Interpretation \\
\midrule
Abrupt change & 76.1 & Abstract algebra & Sudden capability jump \\
Gradual drift & 23.9 & HS mathematics & Continuous improvement \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Cumulative Bayesian DIF}

For longitudinal monitoring, we simulate cumulative Bayesian DIF across 4 successive administrations, where each administration's posterior becomes the next prior.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Cumulative Bayesian DIF power by administration count.}
\label{tab:bayesian}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Administrations & Power ($\delta$=0.8) & Power ($\delta$=1.0) & Power ($\delta$=1.4) & FPR \\
\midrule
1 & 0.22 & 0.41 & 0.68 & 0.048 \\
2 & 0.35 & 0.57 & 0.81 & 0.033 \\
3 & 0.47 & 0.71 & 0.89 & 0.025 \\
4 & 0.54 & 0.77 & 0.93 & 0.022 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

At medium effect size ($\delta=1.0$), single-administration power is only 0.41, but cumulative evidence raises this to 0.71 after 3 administrations and 0.77 after 4. Simultaneously, FPR decreases from 0.048 to 0.022 via Bayesian shrinkage---transient false positives are not reinforced across administrations. Posterior credible interval width reduces by 38\%. This provides a principled framework for longitudinal benchmark monitoring: persistent effects are reliably detected even when individual time points lack power.

%==============================================================================
\section{Toward Benchmark Maintenance}\label{sec:maintenance}

\subsection{Bifactor IRT Equating}

We pilot a principled psychometric framework for item replacement using bifactor IRT with fixed-parameter calibration.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Bifactor model fit and factor structure.}
\label{tab:bifactor}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Factor & Variance explained (\%) & Items loading $>$0.3 \\
\midrule
General & 61.3 & 12,508 \\
Subject-specific (mean) & 15.8 & 219 \\
Subject-specific (min) & 8.2 & 87 \\
Subject-specific (max) & 27.4 & 412 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The bifactor model fits excellently (RMSEA$=$0.019, CFI$=$0.978). The general factor explains 61.3\% of variance, confirming a dominant unidimensional signal (supporting simpler IRT adequacy for ranking). Anchor items (no DIF flag, discrimination$>$0.5, fit residual$<$2.0) comprise 48.8\% of items ($N=$6,107).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Equating quality by replacement rate.}
\label{tab:equating}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Replacement (\%) & $N$ replaced & Spearman $\rho$ & RMSE & Max rank change \\
\midrule
5 & 625 & 0.998 & 0.41 & 23 \\
10 & 1,251 & 0.994 & 0.89 & 54 \\
20 & 2,502 & 0.987 & 1.34 & 89 \\
31 & 3,918 & 0.976 & 1.83 & 127 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

At 31\% replacement (replacing all DIF-flagged items), equated scores maintain $\rho=0.976$ with original rankings. Fixed-parameter calibration converges for 94.2\% of replacement items. Equated benchmarks can refresh the most unstable items with controlled validity loss.

\subsection{Item Generation and Calibration}

We evaluate LLM-based item generation targeting DIF-flagged items, comparing difficulty calibration methods.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Difficulty calibration method comparison for generated items.}
\label{tab:itemgen}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Method & Difficulty $r$ & Discrimination $r$ & Within 0.15 logits (\%) \\
\midrule
Prompt-based targeting & 0.32 & 0.18 & 24 \\
Student simulation & 0.78 & 0.64 & 68 \\
Student sim + iterative & 0.84 & 0.71 & 82 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Student simulation substantially outperforms naive prompt-based generation ($r=0.78$ vs.\ 0.32 for difficulty). Of 1,500 generated replacements for 500 DIF-flagged items, 75.1\% pass quality checks. A holdout panel of 30 models yields $\rho=0.971$ between original and replacement item scores, confirming measurement function preservation. Cognitive level matching reaches 82.4\% (LLMs reproduce factual/comprehension items well but struggle with higher Bloom levels). Total cost: 173 GPU-hours.

\subsection{Graceful Degradation Analysis}

We characterize validity degradation under progressive item replacement with theoretical bounds.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Validity metrics under progressive replacement.}
\label{tab:degradation}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Replacement (\%) & Spearman $\rho$ & SHR & Construct coverage & Max rank $\Delta$ \\
\midrule
0 & 1.000 & 0.9934 & 1.000 & 0 \\
5 & 0.9991 & 0.9928 & 0.997 & 18 \\
10 & 0.9984 & 0.9921 & 0.993 & 42 \\
20 & 0.9962 & 0.9904 & 0.984 & 78 \\
31 & 0.9938 & 0.9886 & 0.972 & 127 \\
50 & 0.9871 & 0.9842 & 0.951 & 203 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

MMLU tolerates $\sim$27\% replacement while maintaining $\rho>0.99$. The critical rate aligns with the empirical finding that 31\% DIF removal yields $\rho=0.996$---replacement with equated items is less disruptive than removal, because equating preserves the dominant general factor. Construct coverage (proportion of domains with $\geq$10 items) stays above 0.95 even at 50\% replacement, reflecting MMLU's 57 subjects averaging 219 items each. Theoretical bounds: $\rho \geq 1 - O(r \cdot (1-\rho^2_\text{anchor}))$ where $r$ is replacement rate and $\rho_\text{anchor}$ is anchor item reliability.

%==============================================================================
\section{Discussion}

\paragraph{Principal finding.} 28--30\% of MMLU items are functionally unstable across model generations in an architecture-homogeneous population with excellent IRT fit. This is not a statistical artifact (sanity check: 0.14\%), not primarily an architecture mixing effect (decoder-only: 29.1\% vs.\ full: 31.3\%), and not fully explained by capability improvement (partial $\rho=0.22$ after controlling domain accuracy). The instability is a structural property of the benchmark-model ecosystem.

\paragraph{Implications for contamination research.} The field's reliance on web-overlap labels for item-level validation has a fundamental blind spot. Our semi-synthetic framework ($\Delta$TPR methodology with cross-subject matching) provides infrastructure for validating any item-level detector. The multi-signal ensemble (AUROC$=$0.82) demonstrates complementary value, but each component operates on different contamination dimensions---no single method is sufficient.

\paragraph{Operational recommendations.} For benchmark maintainers: (1) run temporal DIF as routine diagnostic after each evaluation cycle; (2) use cumulative Bayesian DIF for medium-effect detection; (3) consider equating-based item rotation for the 28--30\% most unstable items; (4) report IRT fit indices alongside benchmark scores. For contamination researchers: (5) validate detectors against semi-synthetic injections, not web-overlap labels; (6) use cross-subject matching to avoid FPR inflation.

\paragraph{Limitations.} The reasoning-RL robustness finding rests on $N=13$ models. ConStat and DVD experiments use smaller subsets than the main DIF analysis. The semi-synthetic injection mechanism (incorrect$\to$correct flipping) simplifies real contamination pathways. Generalization beyond MMLU and GSM8K requires further validation. Higher-order methods (MIMIC, propensity-score matching, multidimensional IRT) may address confounding that our decomposition approach cannot fully resolve.

%==============================================================================
\section{Conclusion}

Psychometric analysis of the METABENCH response matrix reveals that LLM benchmarks harbor structural instabilities invisible to existing contamination detection methodology. Nearly one-third of MMLU items function differently across model generations---a finding that persists after controlling for architecture heterogeneity, IRT model misfit, and aggregate capability improvement. The standard validation approach using web-overlap labels cannot detect this differential signal. We provide a semi-synthetic framework, demonstrate DIF's robustness advantage over per-model detectors under reasoning-RL, and sketch a principled path toward equating-based benchmark maintenance. These findings argue for integrating psychometric monitoring into the benchmark evaluation lifecycle.

